About:
======
Gmail Backup allows to backup and restore the content of your Gmail account. It
uses the IMAP protocol to fetch and store your messages. It can also be used to
migrate your messages between two accounts, for example between your private
inbox and the Google Apps acount.

This project contains the open sourced code of Gmail Backup
(hosted at www.gmail-backup.com).


Status (2026):
==============

This fork has been ported to Python 3 (tested with 3.11 - 3.14) and works with
the current Gmail IMAP service. Python 2 is no longer supported.

Google no longer accepts your normal account password over IMAP. You need to:

  1. Enable IMAP in Gmail: Settings -> See all settings -> Forwarding and
     POP/IMAP -> Enable IMAP.
  2. Turn on 2-Step Verification for your Google account:
     https://myaccount.google.com/signinoptions/twosv
  3. Create an App Password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
     and use it instead of your normal password.


Usage:
======

The command line version needs only Python 3 (no extra packages):

  ./gmail-backup.sh backup  <dir>  you@gmail.com            # asks for password
  ./gmail-backup.sh backup  <dir>  you@gmail.com --stamp    # incremental
  ./gmail-backup.sh restore <dir>  you@gmail.com
  ./gmail-backup.sh list           you@gmail.com
  ./gmail-backup.sh --help

The password can also be given as an argument after the e-mail address or in
the GMAIL_BACKUP_PASSWORD environment variable. Use a name ending with .zip
instead of <dir> to store the backup in a ZIP archive.

The GUI needs wxPython 4 (Phoenix):

  python3 gmail-backup-gui.py


Nix / Lix:
==========

  nix run .                    # command line version
  nix run .#gui                # GUI (wxPython)
  nix build .#gmail-backup     # or .#gmail-backup-gui
  nix develop                  # shell with python3 + wxPython

The flake needs the "nix-command" and "flakes" experimental features.


